commit 41077a004ca3435446204fe969448b4de2890aee
Author: Glen Taggart <glenmtaggart@gmail.com>
Date:   Fri May 22 13:21:23 2026 -0700

    gate slotted-instance raise on the class having free typevars
    
    The instance-side raise for slotted classes without __orig_class__
    storage fired whenever no alias could be recovered. That conflated two
    cases: a genuine bug (a generic factory's alias was dropped) and a
    harmless one (the class has no parametrization to lose).
    
    It broke two legitimate patterns:
      - a non-generic slotted class using @takes_alias instance-side;
      - a concrete slotted subclass, Concrete(Base[int]), where the base's
        typevars are already bound in the MRO.
    
    Both resolve fine straight from type(instance). Gate the raise on
    get_parameters(cls) being non-empty: raise only when the class still
    has free typevars, so dropping the instance alias can actually lose
    information. A subclass that adds its own free typevar, Sub[U](Base[int]),
    still raises -- Sub[str]() really did carry U=str.
    
    Apply the same gate to TypeVarValue.__get__.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

