The class_swap implementation has correctness gaps around preserving the actual constructed type and around pickle/copy reconstruction protocols. These can cause construction failures or silent loss of parametrization in realistic slotted generic classes.

Full review comments:

- [P2] Preserve subclasses returned by constructors — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/review-branch/src/paramsight/ga_proxy.py:92-92
  When `__new__` or a metaclass `__call__` returns a slotted subclass, `cls` is the actual result type, but this line always swaps the instance to a synthetic subclass of the alias origin. That either strips the returned subclass identity and methods when layouts match, or raises a `TypeError` when the subclass adds slots; generic alias construction should preserve the object type the constructor returned while attaching the alias.

- [P2] Override reduce_ex for synthetic classes — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/review-branch/src/paramsight/_class_swap.py:54-54
  If the origin class defines `__reduce_ex__`, the synthetic subclass inherits it and pickle/copy will call that before consulting this `__reduce__`, bypassing `_synth_reduce`. In that case class-swap instances are reconstructed according to the origin's reducer, usually as the origin class without `_paramsight_alias`, so subsequent `@takes_alias`/`TypeVarValue` lookups lose the parametrization despite the strategy claiming copy/pickle survival.

- [P2] Honor new-args during reconstruction — /home/g/wt/paramsight/active/review-branch/src/paramsight/_class_swap.py:73-73
  For a class-swap class whose `__new__` requires arguments, unpickling or copying reaches `_synth_new` and calls `origin.__new__(origin)` with no args, even if the class implements `__getnewargs__`/`__getnewargs_ex__` and is otherwise picklable. This raises `TypeError` and breaks the advertised copy/pickle preservation for slotted immutable or factory-style classes.
